0251: EFB copies executed after the game reused their memory are dropped at the next check

Bug (user report, 2026-10-28, ProjectLM-NX on Switch): the second time the Game Boy Horror map is
opened (Y), its rooms show the foyer's wooden floor instead of flat grey and the Luigi head marker
has wood where its eyes and mouth are. Opening the map copies the 3D view (640x480 RGB565) into a
heap buffer; the game frees that buffer and loads the map's textures into the same memory. Aurora
executes the copy later (frame interpolation replay, another thread), when the map's textures are
already there, so the entry recorded that memory as "the memory under the copy". The memory never
changed afterwards, the entry stayed valid, and every map texture inside the range was decoded
from the copy ("texture ... reads EFB copy memory (1 copies, newest ... 640x480 fmt 0x4): decoding").
Not seen with "EFB copies to RAM" off, nor the first time the map opens (the heap layout differs).

0222 already compares the readback with the memory as the game left it when it issued the copy
(aurora_efb_ram_guest_copy); the entry itself still used the memory as Aurora found it. Now, at every
execution of the copy (repeated replays of its tape included), when the game announced the copy and
has written the range since its newest announcement (and the memory is not our own writeback), the
entry keeps the issue-time hash: the next check (resolve_locked outside replays, or a sweep
requested for the next frame instead of waiting up to 64 frames; the earliest pending request is
kept) drops it and evicts Aurora's copy texture for the destination, as on the console where the
game's writes replaced the copy. The entry still exists for this frame. As with 0250's sweep, the
eviction also removes the destination from the replay slots: an interpolated replay of an earlier
frame that read the copy reads the memory instead (on the console that memory already holds the
game's new data). The 0222 readback logic is unchanged (it keeps comparing with the memory found at
execution). Identity is still per destination and size (the newest announcement), as in 0222.

A game that turned readbacks off (LM-NX on the console) skipped the announcement and its
600 KB hashes; aurora_efb_ram_guest_copy_sparse announces the copy with a sparse fingerprint (32 bytes every
1 KiB and the last 32; a write under 1 KiB falling entirely between two samples is not seen), compared with the same fingerprint at execution; such an announcement is only used for
this check (the 0222 readback ignores it).

